\\ drop-n and array-n fold their literal count in AOT instead of collapsing the
\\ abstract stack to a row.
\\
\\ Their declared variadic effects (`x1..xn n --`, `...elems n -- array`) model a
\\ fixed arity, so before folding the compiler could not tell how many entries the
\\ op consumed. It collapsed to a row, which underflowed (NC.13) whenever concrete
\\ values sat below the consumed span. Folding the literal count recovers the real
\\ arity, so the entries below stay concrete.

\ drop-n drops the top four, preserving three concrete values below.
drop-below: ( a b c d e f g -- a b c ) [ 4 drop-n ] ;

\ array-n packs the top three into an array, preserving four concrete values below.
pack-below: ( a b c d e f g -- a b c d arr ) [ 3 array-n ] ;

\ Runtime results must match the interpreter semantics.
array-len: ( a b c -- n ) [ 3 array-n #len ] ;
array-head: ( a b c -- x ) [ 3 array-n 0 #nth ] ;

\ array-n packs a quotation element; fetching and calling it back must run the body.
array-quot: ( -- n ) [ [ 6 7 * ] 1 array-n 0 #nth call ] ;

1 2 3 4 5 6 7 drop-below + + >string print-line  \ preserved 1 2 3 -> 6
10 20 30 array-len >string print-line            \ [10 20 30] len -> 3
10 20 30 array-head >string print-line           \ [10 20 30] nth 0 -> 10
array-quot >string print-line                    \ [ [6 7 *] ] nth 0 call -> 42
\ The array packs the consumed span, leaving the four below intact.
1 2 3 4 5 6 7 pack-below drop + + + >string print-line  \ drop array, preserved 1 2 3 4 -> 10
